{
  "id": "sein-heissen",
  "level": "A1",
  "title": "Die Verben 'sein' und 'heißen'",
  "group": "Verbs",
  "sourceFile": "seinheissen.html",
  "overview": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "Sein (to be) and heißen (to be called) are essential verbs for identity, descriptions, origin, and names. Sein is highly irregular; heißen follows regular endings except for spelling with ß."
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "content": "",
        "rows": [
          [
            "",
            "sein",
            "heißen"
          ],
          [
            "ich",
            "bin",
            "heiße"
          ],
          [
            "du",
            "bist",
            "heißt"
          ],
          [
            "er/sie/es",
            "ist",
            "heißt"
          ],
          [
            "wir",
            "sind",
            "heißen"
          ],
          [
            "ihr",
            "seid",
            "heißt"
          ],
          [
            "sie/Sie",
            "sind",
            "heißen"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "content": "How they are used"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "content": "sein + noun/adjective: Sie ist Ärztin. Er ist müde.\nsein + place/origin: Wir sind in Berlin. Ich bin aus Köln.\nheißen + name: Ich heiße Anna.\nWie heißt du? / Wie heißen Sie? — What is your name?"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "After sein, the noun remains nominative: Er ist ein Student, not einen Studenten."
      },
      {
        "type": "example",
        "content": "",
        "de": "Ich bin Studentin und heiße Anna.",
        "note": "sein describes identity; heißen gives a name"
      }
    ]
  },
